1

www.nature.com/scientificreports/
attract metal cations. Mineral components on biochar surfaces also improve heavy metal adsorption through
complexation and co-precipitation9,10. Acid or acid-forming treatments can increase the number of available
adsorption sites for Cd on biochar by modifying its surface charge, pore size, and surface area11,12. Phosphoric
acid is commonly used for biochar acidification, as it forms polyphosphate and phosphate cross-bridges, thereby
reducing contraction and shrinkage during the development of porosity13,14. Sha et al.15 demonstrated through
a 90-day incubation experiment that P-modified biochar facilitated the conversion of Cd, Pb, Zn, and Cu from
exchangeable and/or carbonate-bound forms to those bound to organic matter or in residual forms. They
suggested that the mechanisms behind the immobilization of heavy metals by P-modified biochar included
precipitation (involving PO43−, HPO42−, OH−, and CO32−), electrostatic attraction, complexation (with –COOH,
–OH, and R–O–H), and indirect effects resulting from changes in soil parameters like pH, moisture, and
microbial communities. In a separate study, the application of P-modified rice husk and cornstalk biochars
to contaminated soil facilitated the transformation of Cu and Cd from acid-soluble to more stable forms.
Phosphorus compounds in the biochars played a key role in immobilizing the metals through precipitation
or complexation16. In contrast, Rizwan et al.17 observed a low Cd2+ adsorption capacity in biochars derived
from H3PO4-pretreated rice and cotton straws in a contaminated aqueous solution. They concluded that the
adsorption sites on the acidified biochars are occupied by a high concentration of protons, which may hinder
Cd2+ ions from approaching and resulting in low Cd2+ adsorption. Conversely, Dechapanya and Khamwichit18
reported that palm kernel shells biochar activated by H3PO4 exhibited a considerable capacity for Pb+2 removal
from aqueous solutions through functional group complexation and surface precipitation.
Clay minerals have proven effective in adsorbing PTEs from wastewater19. Bentonite clay, in particular, is a
cost-effective option for removing PTEs from soil due to its permanent surface charges, isomorphic substitution
properties, environmental compatibility, and wide availability20. Sun et al.21 found that adding bentonite to
contaminated soil at concentrations of 0%, 0.5%, 1%, 3%, and 5% wt. decreased the exchangeable fraction of
Cd by 11.1%–42.5% and Pb by 20.3%–49.3%. Most of these metals were transformed into unavailable forms,
leading to increases in the residual portions by 3.0%–54.3% for Cd and 6.7%–10.0% for Pb. Similarly, Gao and
Li22 reported that the addition of bentonite to naturally contaminated soil significantly reduced the exchangeable
fractions of Ni and Cu, while the proportions of FeMn oxides and residual fractions increased substantially.
The immobilization process of soil PTEs through amendment materials is complex and influenced by factors
including the type of PTE, the properties of the amendments, and the characteristics of the soil23,24. Studies have
indicated that the combined use of multiple amendments for stabilizing soil PTEs is often more effective than
applying them individually25,26. Due to the cost-effectiveness and efficiency of biochars and bentonite clay in
stabilization of soil Cd, further research is needed to investigate their combined effects on Cd immobilization.
A specific research gap exists regarding the impact of phosphoric acid-modified biochars, in combination with
bentonite clay, on the immobilization of Cd in contaminated calcareous soils. on the other hand, coffee grounds
and municipal solid waste are among the most prevalent and cost-effective types of wastes with varying origins,
in Iran27,28. Moreover, bentonite clay, one of the most abundant and affordable minerals, is found in Iranian
mines29. This is the first study conducted to compare the effects of pristine and H3PO4-modified biochars derived
from coffee grounds and municipal solid waste, in combination with bentonite clay, on chemical fractions and
EDTA-release kinetics of Cd in contaminated calcareous soil and also exploring the underlying soil chemical
mechanisms.

Materials and methods

Soil sampling and polluting

To conduct this research, a composite soil sample was collected from the surface horizon (0–30 cm) of fields at
the College of Agriculture and Natural Resources in Darab, Fars province, southern Iran. The soil was then airdried, sieved through a 2 mm mesh, and various chemical and physical properties were analyzed using standard
laboratory procedures30. A 200-g portion of the soil sample was placed in plastic containers. Then, 100 ml of
an aqueous solution of cadmium, prepared using distilled water and cadmium chloride (CdCl2), was added to
each soil sample and thoroughly mixed to ensure that the amount of Cd introduced was 50 mg kg−1 soil (At this
stage, the soil was nearly saturated, and the solution fully permeated all parts of the sample). The soil samples
were then allowed to dry at room temperature. Once dried, they were softened, and distilled water was added
to bring the moisture content up to field capacity. The samples were then left to dry again at room temperature.
This process of wetting and drying was repeated three times to ensure thorough mixing of the Cd with the soil,
achieve equilibrium, and simulate real field conditions31.
Soil amendments preparation and characterization
The biochars were produced from two types of primary organic materials including coffee grounds and
municipal solid waste using a slow pyrolysis process. This was carried out in an electric furnace at 400 °C for 2
h under limited oxygen conditions32. The biochars were sieved through a 0.5 mm mesh to ensure uniformity. To
acidify the biochars, phosphoric acid (H3PO4) with a normality of 5 N was used. The acidification was carried
out by mixing the biochars with the phosphoric acid in a ratio of 1:10 (grams of biochar to milliliters of acid)33.
The acidified biochars were air-dried and manually homogenized. Various chemical properties of the produced
biochars were then measured using standard laboratory methods as previously described by Boostani et al.34. The
surface functional groups of the biochars were analyzed using Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy
with a Shimadzu DR-8001 instrument and the KBr pellet transmission method. The surface morphology of
the biochar was examined through scanning electron microscopy—Energy Dispersive X-ray Spectroscopy
(SEM–EDX) using a TESCAN VEGA3 instrument from the Czech Republic. Bentonite was sourced from the
Pharmaceutical Chemicals Industries Complex of Dr. Mojallali in Iran. The bentonite had a cation exchange
capacity (CEC) of 75.4 cmol(+) kg−1, an electrical conductivity (EC) (1:20) of 1.00 dS m−1, and a pH (1:20) of
