www.nature.com/scientificreports/
8.20. Additionally, the concentrations of soluble K, Na, and Ca in the bentonite were 64.6, 278, and 124 mg L−1,
respectively.

Experimental design and incubation experiment

The study was performed as a factorial experiment within a completely randomized design, with three
replications. The experimental factors included bentonite at three levels (0% wt. (B0), 1% wt. (B1), and 2% wt.
(B2)) and five types of biochar treatments: no biochar (Cl), coffee grounds biochar (G), coffee grounds biochar
modified with phosphoric acid (GH), municipal solid waste biochar (M), and municipal solid waste biochar
modified with phosphoric acid (MH), each applied at a 2% wt. level. Contaminated soil samples (50 mg Cd
kg−1) in plastic containers were thoroughly mixed with the biochar and bentonite according to the experimental
design. The soil moisture was adjusted to near field capacity using distilled water. The samples were maintained
at room temperature (25 ± 2 °C) for 90 days, with soil moisture kept at field capacity through daily adjustments
with distilled water. After the incubation period, the soil samples were air-dried, passed through a 2 mm sieve,
and stored in the laboratory for chemical analysis.

Cadmium separation in different soil chemical fractions

Soil Cd was separated into five distinct fractions using the method outlined by Salbu and Krekling35. This
technique categorizes Cd into five specific forms: soluble plus exchangeable (WsEx), carbonate bound (Car),
bound to iron-manganese oxides (FeMnOx), bound to organic materials (OM) and residual (Res). The Res
fraction of soil Cd was determined by subtracting the combined total of the other fractions from the soil’s total
Cd content. Total Cd of the soil was assessed using the approach described by Sposito et al.36. Additionally, the
mobility factor of Cd (MF) in the soil was calculated according to the method of Salbu and Krekling35 as follows:
((WsEx + Car) /sum of ﬁve fractions) × 100.

Cadmium desorption experiment

For Cd desorption studies, 5 g of air-dried soil were extracted with 25 mL of 0.01M EDTA at pH 7.037 using
an end-over-end circulatory shaker for time intervals ranging from 5 to 1440 min (5, 15, 30, 60, 120, 360, 720,
and 1440 min) at a temperature of 25 ± 2 °C. The samples were then immediately centrifuged at 3000 rpm for 15
min. The supernatants were filtered through Whatman 42 filter paper, and Cd concentrations were measured
using atomic absorption spectroscopy (AAS) (PG 990, PG Instruments Ltd. UK). The cumulative release of Cd
(q) over time (t) was analyzed using a power function kinetic model (qt = etf ). The evaluation of the fitted kinetic
model was based on relatively high values of the coefficient of determination (r2) and low values of the standard
error of estimate (SEE).

Data analysis

Data were statistically analyzed using MSTATC software. Mean comparisons were conducted using Duncan’s
test at a 5% probability level, and graphs were created with Excel 2013. Pearson correlation coefficients were
calculated using SPSS 12.0 software.

