Table 1 outlines the physicochemical properties of the soil prior to contamination. The soil texture consisted of
44.8% sand, 39.7% silt, and 15.5% clay, categorizing it as loam textural class. With a pH of 7.40 and an electrical
conductivity (EC) of 1.40 dS m−1, the soil is characterized as alkaline and non-saline in nature. Additionally,
the soil also contains 43.7% calcium carbonate equivalent (CCE) and 1.50% organic matter (OM). These
characteristics are typical of calcareous soils found in southern Iran38. The total Cd concentration in the soil was
below the detection limit by the AAS.

Chemical characteristics of the biochars

The chemical properties of the biochars used in this study are presented in Table 2. Among them, the M biochar
exhibited the highest pH value (8.50), which can be attributed to its elevated ash content (82.0%) compared to

