Table 2. Some physical and chemical properties of the biochars. G, coffee grounds biochar; GH, coffee
grounds biochar modified with phosphoric acid; M, municipal solid waste biochar; MH, municipal solid waste
biochar modified with phosphoric acid; CEC, cation exchange capacity; EC, electrical conductivity; Nd, nondetectable.

the G biochar (3.20%) (Table 2). The modification of the biochars with phosphoric acid resulted in a significant
reduction in their pH levels, while slightly increasing their phosphorus content and CEC values (Table 2). The
G and GH biochars demonstrated higher carbon content than the M and MH biochars. Additionally, nitrogen
concentrations were significantly lower in the M and MH biochars compared to the G and GH biochars (Table
2), which is likely due to the organic nature of the coffee grounds used as feedstocks. Furthermore, the cadmium
content in all the biochars was below the detection limit of the AAS (Table 2).
Table 3. Effects of biochars and bentonite levels on the Cd content in the soil chemical fractions (mg kg−1)
and Cd mobility factor (%) in a contaminated calcareous soil. C, control; G, coffee grounds biochar; GH, coffee
grounds biochar modified with phosphoric acid; M, municipal solid waste biochar; MH, municipal solid waste
biochar modified with phosphoric acid; B0, without bentonite addition; B1, bentonite application at a rate of 1%
wt.; B2, bentonite application at a rate of 2% wt. WsEx, water soluble and exchangeable fraction; Car, bound to
carbonates; FeMnOx, bound to iron-manganese oxides; Res, residual fraction; MF, mobility factor. *Numbers
followed by same letters in each column and rows, in each section, are not significantly (P < 0.05) different.

The addition of bentonite did not significantly alter the soil Cd concentration in the FeMnOx fraction on
its own. In close agreement with our findings, Sun et al.21 investigated the impact of varying concentrations
of bentonite (0.5%, 1%, 3%, and 5% wt.) on Cd-contaminated soil. Their findings revealed that none of the
bentonite treatments significantly influenced the Cd content in the FeMnOx fraction of the soil compared
to the control. However, among the biochars used, the addition of GH and MH, in the absence of bentonite,
resulted in a significant increase in this fraction by 14.6% and 24.8%, respectively, compared to the control
(Table 3). The application of biochars to soils contaminated with PTEs has consistently been shown to result
in an increase in the concentration of PTEs within the FeMnOx fraction of the soil59,60. The formation of Feoxide-organic matter composites enhances the negative charge on the surface of Fe-oxides, thereby facilitating
the adsorption of cationic metals onto these surfaces61. In this study, the incorporation of biochars into the soil
appeared to facilitate the formation of biochar-FeMn-oxide composites, leading to increased Cd adsorption
onto soil FeMn-oxides. On the other hands, phosphate ions can chemically interact with iron oxides by forming
surface complexes with one or two singly coordinated surface groups (≡FeOH−0.5). The formation reaction for
the predominant bidentate surface phosphate species can be expressed as follows62:
2 ≡ FeOH−0.5 + PO43− + 2H+ → (≡ FeO)2 PO2−
2 + 2H2 O

As a result, the adsorption of phosphate by iron oxides can substantially increase the surface negative charge of
these oxides, which in turn enhances the adsorption of cations, such as Cd2⁺. This phenomenon may explain
the higher Cd content in the FeMnOx fraction of soils treated with P-modified biochars. In soils treated with
various biochars, increasing bentonite levels from B0 to B2 did not produce consistent effects on the changes in
Cd content within the FeMnOx fraction (Table 3).
The incorporation of all the biochars into the soil led to a significant rise in soil Cd content in the Res fraction
compared to the control, when no bentonite was applied. In this line, the G treatment had the highest increase by
29.8% (Table 3). Furthermore, increasing bentonite levels from B0 to B2, without biochar addition, significantly
elevated soil Cd concentration in the Res fraction by 15.1% (Table 3). In a study by Rahimi et al.63, the addition
of Ca-bentonite and biochar derived from grape pruning residues at 1% and 4% wt. to a Cd-contaminated
Scientific Reports |

(2025) 15:14301

| https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-99120-7

7

www.nature.com/scientificreports/
calcareous soil similarly raised the soil Cd content in the Res fraction. Gao and Li22 reported that the Ni and
Cu contents in the Res and FeMnOx fractions of mining soil were significantly increased with enhancing the
bentonite levels. They concluded that bentonite could reduce the biological toxicity and mobility of heavy metals
in contaminated soil, making it a recommended stabilizer for heavy metal pollution. On the other hand, when
the soil was treated with G and GH biochars, increasing bentonite from B0 to B2 led to a significant decrease in
soil Cd content in the Res fraction. In contrast, for soils treated with M and MH biochars, Cd content in this
fraction significantly increased with higher bentonite levels (Table 3).
The mobility factor index of PTEs in soil indicates their bioavailability and leachability. The higher value
of this index corresponds to the higher mobility of soil PTEs64. The mobility factor is calculated using the
data derived from a sequential extraction procedure and is applied to compare the efficacy of different soil
amendments on the PTEs immobilization65. The effect of varying bentonite levels on changing the mobility
factor in all the biochar treatments were different. In the absence of biochar, increasing the bentonite levels from
B0 to B2 caused a significant decrease in the mobility factor of soil Cd by 15.6%, whereases in the G and GH
biochar treatment, this effect was reverse and there was an antagonism effect between coffee grounds biochars
and bentonite (Table 3). However, the mobility factor of soil Cd decreased more significantly in the M and MH
biochar treatments in the presence of bentonite at both levels compared to the alone application of bentonite
(B1: 42.7% VS 39.5%, B2: 42.6% VS 40.2%) (Table 3). The interaction effects of treatments showed that the most
effective combination treatments for decreasing the soil Cd mobility factor compared to the control were the
G + B0 and M + B2, with reduction of 25.7% and 21%, respectively (Table 3). The soil Cd content in the fractions
of Res (r = −0.97∗∗ , P < 0.01) and FeMnOx (r = −0.51∗∗ , P < 0.01) had negative significant correlation
with the soil Cd mobility factor. In addition, there were positive significant correlation amongst soil Cd content
in the WsEx (r = +0.88∗∗ , P < 0.01) and Car (r = +0.72∗∗ , P < 0.01) fractions with the mobility factor
of soil Cd. This shows that application of bentonite and biochar could immobilize Cd in the contaminated
calcareous soil through conversion of bioavailable forms of soil Cd into the fractions with lower mobility and
leachability. Matin et al.59 with application of biochars generated from almond and walnut shell in combination
with nano-clay into an alkaline contaminated soil concluded that this amendment shifted the exchangeable
Desorption of Cd from treated soils

Figure 3 shows that 83.5% of Cd was released from soils treated with bentonite and biochars within the first two
hours using an EDTA solution, indicating a two-phase desorption process. A rapid initial release followed by a
significant decline in the desorption rate until equilibrium is achieved. Similar two-phase desorption patterns for
PTEs in biochar- and clay-amended contaminated soils have been reported in previous studies34,44,66. The initial
stage of Cd desorption from the treated soils is likely associated with the Cd chemical fractions characterized
by lower adsorption energy and greater mobility, such as Cd-WsEx and Cd-Car. In contrast, the second stage
of desorption appears to correspond to more stable Cd fractions in the soil, including Cd-FeMnOx and CdRes31,67. The lowest amount of Cd release from the soil over 24 h was observed in the combined treatment of
M + B2 (22.4 mg Cd kg−1 soil), representing a 18.7% reduction compared to the C + B0 treatment. Additionally,
this treatment demonstrated strong performance in reducing the soil Cd mobility index (39.9%), second only
to the G + B0 treatment, which achieved a 37.5% reduction (Table 3). Among the biochars, M exhibited the
highest ash content (82.0%) and pH value (8.50) (Table 2). The highest soil pH (7.94) was recorded in soils
treated with the combined application of M and B2. These findings suggest that the primary mechanism of Cd
immobilization in the M + B2 treatment is likely sedimentation, resulting in a slower release of Cd from the
sediment phase by EDTA solution.

Fig. 3. Cumulative soil Cd desorption (extracted by EDTA) (mg kg−1) as affected by biochars and bentonite
levels. Notes: C, control; G, coffee grounds biochar; GH, coffee grounds biochar modified with phosphoric
acid; M, municipal solid waste biochar; MH, municipal solid waste biochar modified with phosphoric acid; B0,
without bentonite addition; B1, bentonite application at a rate of 1% wt.; B2, bentonite application at a rate of
2% wt.

