                                  Fitting of Cd release data to power function kinetics model
                                  The desorption of soil Cd using an EDTA solution followed the power function kinetics model, with R2 values
                                  between 0.80 and 0.99 and SEE values ranging from 0.03 to 0.09. This supports previous studies that have
                                  identified the power function kinetics model as the most suitable for describing PTEs desorption in calcareous
                                  soils34,68,69. The power function equation is expressed as: qt = etf or ln qt = ln e + f ln t, where, qt represents the
                                  quantity of Cd desorbed at time t, ‘‘e’’ denotes the initial Cd desorption rate (mg Cd kg−1 h−1)b and ‘‘f ’’ is the
                                  desorption rate coefficient corresponding to the slope of the equation (mg Cd kg−1)−1. The main effects of
                                  biochars, as well as the interactions between biochar and bentonite levels, on the constants "e" and "f " were
                                  statistically significant at p ≤ 0.01. However, the main effects of bentonite levels alone were not significant for
                                  these parameters (Table 4). As reported by Dang et al.37, in this kinetic model, a decrease in the parameter
                                  "e" and an increase in "f " signify a reduction in the rate of heavy metal desorption from the soil. The M + B2
                                  combined treatment resulted in the greatest decrease in the "e" constant (-29.8%) and the highest increase in
                                  the "f " constant (+ 40.0%), indicating that this combination minimizes soil Cd desorption rate. Dang et al.37
                                  observed that when the constant "f " in the power function kinetic model is less than or equal to 0.25, it indicates
                                  the involvement of multiple diffusion sites in Zn desorption from the soil. In this study, the "f " value was below
                                  0.25 for all treatments, confirming the role of multiple diffusion sites in Cd desorption, consistent with previous
                                  research by Boostani et al.44, highlighting varying bond energy and reactivity in soil Cd release. In the power
                                  function equation, differentiating qt with respect to t, yields dq/dt = eftf−1. When t = 1, this simplifies to dq/dt = ef,
                                  where “ef ” represents the initial desorption rate of a soil element70. The interaction effects between biochar and
                                  bentonite levels on the “ef ” constant were statistically significant at P ≤ 0.01. The greatest reduction in the initial
                                  desorption rate of soil Cd was also observed in the combined treatment of M + B2 by 11.6% compared to the
                                  control (Table 4).
                                      Table 5 shows correlations between the power function model parameters, Cd content in various soil
                                  fractions, and soil properties. The 'e' parameter positively correlated with Cd in the WsEx fraction and MF index,
                                  but negatively with Cd in the Res fraction and available phosphorus. The ‘ef ’ parameter negatively correlated


